values
  ('when-you-inherit-money-you-did-not-expect', 'When You Inherit Money You Did Not Expect', 'Money arrives because somebody died, and nothing about having it feels straightforward. Why it sits so oddly, what to do with it in the first year, and how to handle the people around it.', 'A letter arrives, or a lawyer calls, and there is a sum of money that is now yours. More than you have had before, or enough to change something, or enough that you have had to look at it twice.

And instead of relief there is a kind of static. You cannot be straightforwardly pleased, because of where it came from, and you cannot be straightforwardly sad, because there is money on the table. Most people find they are not able to feel either thing cleanly.

Why it sits so strangely

It is worth knowing that almost nobody experiences this simply, including people who badly needed the money.

The two things arrive together. The loss and the gain are the same event, so every good feeling about one is interrupted by the other, and there is no order in which to take them.

It can feel like payment. Something in the arithmetic reads as an exchange, and that is uncomfortable even when you know it is nonsense.

It arrives at the worst possible time for decisions. Grief in the first months does specific things to judgment, attention and memory, and that is exactly when the paperwork demands answers.

And it is often entangled with the person. Money from somebody who was careful with it, or chaotic with it, or who you fought with about it, carries all of that into your account along with the balance.

The first year

Almost every piece of good advice about inherited money is some version of slow down.

Put it somewhere boring and leave it. A savings account you cannot easily reach. The decision not to decide is itself a decision, and it is usually the right one for the first six to twelve months.

Deal only with what has a deadline. Tax, probate paperwork, anything the estate actually requires. The rest can wait, and most of it will look different later.

Expect to want to do something dramatic. Quit, move, give it all away, spend it fast so it stops sitting there. Those impulses are common and they are usually about the grief rather than about the money.

And get one piece of professional advice before anything large. An accountant or a financial planner, once, paid for by the hour. The tax position on inherited money is not intuitive and the cost of getting it wrong is larger than the fee.

What to do about the guilt

This is the part people do not say out loud, and it is almost universal.

Notice whether you are refusing to use it as a form of loyalty. Plenty of people leave an inheritance untouched for years because spending it feels like agreeing that the person is gone.

Ask what they would have wanted, and be honest about the answer. For most people, the answer is that they wanted you to have it and use it. That is usually the whole point of leaving it.

Consider spending a small part of it deliberately and visibly on something good. Many people find this is what breaks the deadlock, and it does not have to be large.

And separate the guilt from any actual wrong. If nothing unfair happened, the discomfort is grief wearing a different coat, not a verdict on you.

The people around it

Money reshapes families, and this is where most of the lasting damage happens.

Keep the amount private, including from people who ask directly. You are not obliged to say, and once it is known it cannot be unknown.

Expect requests, and decide your position before one arrives rather than in the moment. Having a settled answer makes it much easier to say.

Be careful with early gifts to family. Generosity in the first months is often grief looking for somewhere to go, and it sets a precedent you may not want.

And where there is a sibling who got a different amount, that is a conversation worth having directly and early. Silence on this reliably turns into a decade of resentment.

If the money is tangled with the relationship

Sometimes the inheritance is from somebody you were not close to, or who hurt you, or who you had not spoken to in years.

That version is harder, not easier, because the money arrives with an unfinished relationship attached and no one left to settle it with.

You are allowed to take it. Accepting money is not forgiveness, it is not a statement about them, and it does not revise the history.

And you are allowed to use it for something entirely your own, without any gesture toward their memory, if that is what you want.

When to talk to someone

If the money has become the main thing you think about, if you cannot make any decision at all, if you are spending in a way that frightens you, or if the grief has not moved at all since it arrived, that is worth taking to somebody.

A therapist is useful here because the money is rarely the real subject, and grief that is stuck around an object or a sum is a recognizable thing rather than an unusual one. Your doctor can point you toward local grief services, many of them free. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Money', 'Grief']::text[], 0, '2026-11-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-inherit-money-you-did-not-expect.jpg', 'Rows of worn old books on a shelf beside a framed picture.', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/5917107/free-old-books-stacked-messy-shelf-photo'),
  ('when-you-cannot-picture-the-future-at-all', 'When You Cannot Picture the Future at All', 'Asked where you will be in a year, you get nothing, and that blankness is frightening. What it usually means, why it is not a prediction, and how to work while it lasts.', 'Somebody asks what you want to be doing next year and there is nothing there. Not a bad picture. No picture. You can describe tomorrow and possibly next week, and past that the screen is blank.

For a lot of people this is more alarming than feeling low, because it seems like information. It feels as though you have looked ahead and found nothing waiting.

What the blankness usually is

It is worth saying plainly: it is a symptom far more often than it is a forecast.

Imagining the future is a mental task, and it takes energy. Below a certain level people stop being able to do it, the same way they stop being able to follow a film or read a page. The machinery is tired, and the output goes blank.

It also runs on memory. Picturing a plausible next year is mostly assembled from remembered scenes, and a stretch of low mood flattens those, which leaves less to build with.

And it needs some expectation that what you do will matter. When that has gone quiet, the exercise stops making sense rather than returning a bad answer.

None of that is the same as there being nothing ahead. It is the difference between a camera being broken and a room being empty.

Why it feels so convincing

Because blankness does not announce itself as a malfunction. It presents as a result.

There is no visible error. A low mood you can argue with. An absence gives you nothing to push against, so it feels neutral and therefore trustworthy.

It matches the other evidence you are collecting at the time, which is already slanted.

And people around you ask future questions constantly, so you are reminded of it several times a week without ever being able to answer.

Working while it lasts

The useful move is to stop trying to see further and change the distance you are working at.

Shorten the horizon on purpose. What is happening this week. What is happening tomorrow morning. That is not a failure of ambition, it is the right scale for current conditions.

Postpone anything large and reversible-looking that you can postpone. Quitting, ending, moving, selling. Decisions made from a blank screen tend to be regretted, and nothing is lost by waiting.

Keep the appointments and commitments you already have. They are a structure built by a version of you with more information, and letting them lapse removes the scaffolding.

And answer the future question with something honest and small. I am not thinking much past Christmas at the moment. Most people accept that immediately.

What tends to bring it back

Worth knowing, because it does come back, and usually not by being thought about.

It returns gradually and in small things first. Wanting to see somebody next month, having an opinion about a trip, getting mildly interested in a plan. People rarely notice it returning and usually notice afterward that it has.

It tracks the other things. Sleep, eating, movement, the dose being right, whatever was going on at the time. The imagination is downstream of all of that and it recovers with them rather than ahead of them.

And doing a few things that have mattered before helps more than reflecting on it. Borrowed scaffolding works while your own is down.

What not to do with it

Do not treat it as a decision about your life. It is a reading taken with a broken instrument, and acting on it is the main risk here.

Do not argue with it directly. Trying to force a picture of next year tends to produce more blankness and a sense of failure on top.

And do not take it as evidence about who you are. This is one of the most common experiences in low mood and one of the least talked about, which is exactly why it feels so personal.

When to tell somebody

This is worth raising with a doctor or a therapist rather than waiting for it to pass, particularly if it has lasted more than a couple of weeks or arrived with the other things that tend to travel with it.

Say it in plain words, because clinicians recognize it immediately and it is useful information for them. I cannot picture anything ahead is a sentence worth using.

And if the blankness has turned into something more active, a sense that there is no point, or thoughts of not being here, tell your doctor or a therapist today rather than waiting for the next appointment. That is not an overreaction, it is the thing to do, and it is far more common than people realize. In the US you can call or text 988 at any time.', array['Depression', 'Decision Making']::text[], 0, '2026-11-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-cannot-picture-the-future-at-all.jpg', 'A glacier stretching toward mountains half hidden by low cloud.', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/3290390/free-photo-image-cc0-cloud-pictures-images-cold'),
  ('cooking-for-someone-who-cannot-eat-much', 'Cooking for Someone Who Cannot Eat Much', 'You make something and it comes back untouched, and it stings more than it should. Why feeding carries so much, what actually helps when appetite is small, and how to stop keeping score.', 'You made the thing they used to like. It came back with two forkfuls gone, and you took the plate away and said it was fine, and then stood in the kitchen feeling something considerably larger than the occasion seemed to warrant.

Illness, treatment, age, grief, medication. There are many reasons somebody stops being able to eat much, and almost none of them have anything to do with your cooking.

Why feeding carries so much

Worth naming, because people in this position are often surprised by how much it hurts.

It is one of the few things you can still do. When most of what is happening is outside your control, cooking is concrete, and a refused plate is a refused piece of helping.

It reads as a verdict. Logically you know it is not. The part of you that cooked it does not entirely believe that.

It is also a running measurement. Every meal produces a number, and when the number keeps being low, there is nowhere for that to go except into you.

And feeding somebody is close to the oldest form of caring there is, which is why this lands somewhere deeper than a practical disappointment.

What actually helps when appetite is small

Most of the useful adjustments are the opposite of what instinct suggests.

Make the portion embarrassingly small. A large plate is discouraging before anybody has started, and a small one that gets finished is better for both of you than a big one that does not.

Offer food more often rather than in bigger amounts. Six small chances beat three proper meals when capacity is low.

Go for cold, bland and easy before anything rich. Smell is often the problem, and anything strongly scented can end the attempt before the first bite. Cold food smells less.

Keep things within reach that need no preparation. Yogurt, crackers, fruit, a drink with something in it. A lot of what gets eaten is eaten because it happened to be there at the one moment the appetite appeared.

And follow the window rather than the clock. Appetite in illness often arrives at odd hours and does not last. Meeting it when it comes matters more than when dinner is supposed to be.

Taking the pressure out of it

This is the part that changes the atmosphere, and the atmosphere is doing more than the recipe.

Put the food down and go. Watching is well meant and it is the single most appetite-killing thing in the room.

Stop asking how much they have had. They know, they are usually already uncomfortable about it, and the question makes the next meal harder.

Do not negotiate. Three more bites turns eating into a transaction with a loser, and it costs you more than it gains.

And let them refuse without a reaction. The fastest way to make somebody dread meals is to make each one an event with your disappointment at the end.

Looking after your own end of it

Because this grinds people down and it is rarely acknowledged.

Separate the cooking from the outcome. You can control what is offered, how often, and how pleasant it is to be near. You cannot control how much goes in, and holding yourself to that measure is a losing arrangement.

Find another way to give the thing you are trying to give. Sitting with them, a drink made properly, a cold washcloth, reading aloud. The impulse behind the cooking can go somewhere that does not get returned on a plate.

And say it out loud to somebody. Most people carrying this have never mentioned it, because it sounds trivial next to what the other person is going through, and it is not trivial.

Getting the practical help that exists

Ask the medical team directly rather than working it out alone. Appetite is a standard thing to raise, and there is often something specific available: a change in timing, a medication adjustment, supplement drinks, or a referral to a dietitian who does exactly this.

Ask about anything that makes eating physically hard. A sore mouth, swallowing trouble, nausea, constipation. These are treatable and they are frequently the actual obstacle.

When to get support for yourself

If the meals have become the thing you dread, if you are arguing about food, if you are exhausted in a way that sleep does not touch, or if you have started to feel that you are failing at the one job you took on, that is worth taking somewhere.

A therapist who works with caregivers can help, and so can a caregiver support group, which many people dismiss and then find is the most useful hour of their month. Your doctor is the right person to tell if this has started to affect your own health, and can point you toward both.', array['Food', 'Caregiving']::text[], 0, '2026-11-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/cooking-for-someone-who-cannot-eat-much.jpg', 'Small blue and white patterned bowls stacked on a shelf.', 'Photo by Travel Photographer via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/stacked-bowls-GAMHRIGNDW'),
  ('describing-what-happened-without-the-story', 'Describing What Happened Without the Story', 'Most of what you replay is interpretation wearing the clothes of fact. How to separate the two, why it lowers the temperature, and what to do when the story turns out to be right.', 'She did not answer my message becomes she is annoyed with me becomes I have done something wrong again. Three separate things, and by the evening they have fused into one, and what you are now upset about is not the thing that happened.

This is one of the few mental habits that is genuinely worth drilling, because almost everything else gets easier once it is in place.

The difference, precisely

A description is what a camera would record. An interpretation is what it means. Both are necessary, and the trouble starts when the second is stored as the first.

He raised his voice is a description. He was furious with me is an interpretation.

Nobody has replied in the group is a description. They are leaving me out is an interpretation.

My chest is tight and my hands are cold is a description. Something is wrong with my heart is an interpretation.

The test is whether another person in the room would have written down the same sentence. Most of what we carry around would not survive that.

Why separating them lowers the temperature

It is not about deciding the interpretation is wrong. Often it is right. The point is that an interpretation can be examined and a fact cannot.

The description is almost always smaller. What actually happened fits in one line, and the thing that has been running for two hours does not.

You stop defending a position you never consciously adopted. Interpretations get held with the certainty of observations, and separating them restores the ordinary ability to be unsure.

And the feeling drops, usually noticeably. Not because you have argued yourself out of it, but because the thing it was attached to turned out to be smaller.

How to actually do it

This works far better written than thought about, at least at first.

Write the event in one sentence, with nothing in it that a camera could not see or a recorder could not catch. No adverbs, no motives, no history.

Then write everything else underneath, in a second list, labeled as what you are making of it. You are not deleting that list. You are putting it where it belongs.

Include your own body in the description. Jaw tight, face hot, hands shaking. Those are facts and they tend to get left out, which makes the feeling seem to come from the situation rather than through you.

And notice the words that smuggle in meaning. Always, never, obviously, deliberately, again. Each of those is an interpretation in the costume of a detail.

When it is hardest

Some situations defeat this, and it helps to know which.

Anything involving somebody you have a long history with, because the history arrives automatically and feels like part of what you saw.

Anything where you are already tired or hungry, because the separation takes effort that is not available.

And anything that has been running for days, because by then the story has been rehearsed enough to feel like a memory of what happened.

In those cases the realistic aim is one line of description, not a full account. One is enough to interrupt it.

What to do if the story turns out to be right

This is the part people expect to be a problem and it is usually the opposite.

A confirmed interpretation gives you something to act on. They were annoyed, now known rather than suspected, is a thing you can address directly.

You will have spent far less on it. The hours lost to this are mostly spent in the uncertain middle, and getting to the answer sooner is a saving regardless of the answer.

And being right about one of them does not retroactively make the habit unnecessary. The point was never that your readings are usually wrong. It was that you cannot tell which are which while they are fused.

When to take it further

A certain amount of this is ordinary and everyone does it.

It is worth bringing to someone if the replaying is costing you sleep, if you cannot get any distance from the interpretations even when you try, if other people''s neutral behavior reliably reads as rejection, or if the stories are mostly about what is wrong with you.

That is well-trodden ground for a therapist, and this particular skill is one they teach directly rather than something you have to arrive at alone. Your doctor can point you toward what is available if you are not sure where to start.', array['DBT', 'Mindfulness']::text[], 0, '2026-11-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/describing-what-happened-without-the-story.jpg', 'Fallen leaves resting on the still surface of a puddle.', 'Photo by Matt Bango via StockSnap, CC0', 'https://stocksnap.io/photo/fall-leaves-RIBBRLSY7G'),
  ('when-you-cannot-tell-hunger-from-anxiety', 'When You Cannot Tell Hunger From Anxiety', 'The same hollow feeling could be hunger, nerves or tiredness, and reading it wrong sends the day sideways. Why the signals overlap, and a few ways to tell them apart in the moment.', 'There is a feeling in your middle. It could be that you have not eaten since seven. It could be the conversation you have at four. It could be that you slept badly. You genuinely cannot tell, and whichever one you guess, you will act on it.

Guess wrong and you eat when you were nervous, or you push through a meeting with nothing in you since breakfast, and the afternoon goes worse than it needed to.

Why the signals overlap

This is not a failure of attention. The two states are genuinely similar from the inside.

They use some of the same equipment. Both produce changes in the stomach, both can make you shaky and light-headed, and both make it harder to concentrate.

They also happen together. Being anxious often means not eating, and not eating makes anxiety worse, so by the afternoon you usually have both and no way to tell which is louder.

The reading gets quieter with disuse. People who have spent years eating by the clock, or ignoring the signal to get through a shift, end up with a less distinct sense of it, and that is reversible rather than permanent.

And a few things blur it directly. Caffeine on an empty stomach produces something almost identical to both. So do several medications, poor sleep and a long stretch of stress.

Things that tell them apart

None of these is perfect and together they do fairly well.

Check the clock before the feeling. If it has been four or five hours, hunger is probably in the mix regardless of what else is going on. This one small fact resolves most cases.

Notice where it is and what it does. Hunger tends to be lower, hollow, and comes in waves that build. Anxiety tends to be higher in the chest and throat, more constant, and often arrives with a thought attached.

Look for the thought. Anxiety usually comes with a subject, even a vague one. Hunger does not have an opinion about this afternoon.

See whether it responds to a small amount of food. A few bites of something plain settles hunger noticeably within ten minutes and does very little for anxiety. That is a useful test rather than a meal.

And ask what time of day it is. Mid-afternoon is when these two most commonly get confused, because it is when both are most likely to be present at once.

What to do when you still cannot tell

The answer is usually to treat the hunger, because it is the cheaper mistake.

Eat something small and plain anyway. If it was hunger, it is dealt with. If it was anxiety, you have lost nothing and you are now better equipped for it. Facing an anxious afternoon with nothing in you is the version that goes badly.

Then do one short physical thing. Stand up, go outside, cold water on your wrists. If something was anxiety, this is where it shows, and it separates the two faster than thinking about it.

And give it ten minutes before concluding anything. Both of these change over that span and in different directions, which is itself the clearest signal you will get.

Getting the reading back

If this has been blurred for a long time, it does come back, and the method is dull and effective.

Eat at roughly regular times for a few weeks, even without a clear signal. Steady intake makes the real hunger signal easier to pick out against the background.

Pause briefly before eating and note what you notice, without changing the decision. You are collecting readings rather than enforcing anything.

And keep an eye on caffeine, which is the single most common thing muddying this for people who cannot work out why they feel hollow and jittery at eleven.

When it is worth raising with somebody

This is ordinary and extremely common, particularly in people who are stressed or busy.

It is worth mentioning to a doctor if you have lost weight without meaning to, if eating is regularly painful or uncomfortable, or if the shakiness and light-headedness are severe or happen even after eating, since there are physical causes worth ruling out.

And it is worth taking to a therapist if the confusion has become a source of anxiety in itself, if you are regularly going whole days without eating, or if decisions about food have started to feel loaded and difficult. Both of those are workable, and the earlier they are raised the simpler the work tends to be.', array['Body', 'Grounding']::text[], 0, '2026-11-26T14:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz, '/assets/articles/when-you-cannot-tell-hunger-from-anxiety.jpg', 'Two bowls of oatmeal on a counter, with syrup poured over one.', 'Photo via rawpixel, CC0', 'https://www.rawpixel.com/image/3283291/free-photo-image-coffee-breakfast-granola-bowl')
